his house and its entire contents have  been reduced to ashes and rubble pasta  Samuel choco Anika is still struggling  to comprehend the brutality of the  attack at the end of April  dozens of nomadic cattle herders  attacked the village of Nimbo killing 20  people the pasta was lucky to escape  with his life if I won't believe it why  would you like they ambushed us and set  fire to the house we couldn't go outside  because the attackers were still out  there and we would give up water so my  wife my daughter and I lay down on the  floor so as not to catch fire it was  terrible  since the attack the village has been  under heavy guard  the villagers fear further attacks the  attackers are believed to have been  herdsmen from the Fulani ethnic group  for years now more and more Fulani have  been pushing into the Fertile south of  Nigeria from the arid north we are  talking to a Fulani herdsmen when we see  how quickly the situation can escalate  an angry farmer has discovered a cow in  his field  an apology comes the situation which is  it a human haka imam ooh-rah we don't  want to cause any trouble either but  sometimes it happens that a cow straight  onto a field some of us don't speak  English they don't know how to react  sometimes they just try to run away  comida sinaia by RI Morgan I tried being  difficult coolly between 2011 and 2015  there have been some 400 violent  incidents between herdsmen and farmers  according to local media more than 1000  people have been killed and the  numbers are rising many Nigerians fear  that the traditional conflict between  some herdsmen and farmers has the  potential to turn into a serious  national crisis it's not only a question  of fertile soil and grassland but also  of deeper political conflicts in a  country that faces many ethnic and  religious challenges a local government  spokesperson in the state capital  Enugu warns that some people are  exploiting the attacks to increase  ethnic divisions in Nigeria the long  running conflict between the Muslim  north and the Christian South is rearing  its head again Nigerian President  Muhammadu Buhari who has been in office  for a year is Fulani from the north of  the country he has faced criticism since  the recent attacks and since  independence today there was no problem  they have been going around he's just  that maybe there felt that is a change  of government as favorable to them I'd  have to go off the normal way they've  been doing pasta chokku Anika and his  family reject political hate speech and  all talk of revenge they are praying for  peace and are thankful that a neighbor  has taken them in and they say they will  continue to welcome peaceful herdsmen in  the future
 

